            "creation_time": "2026-11-14T22:09:14Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "bug_id": 19322,
            "text": "x$name on a list calls pstrmatch() for each name until one matches, and every call does vmaxget(), translateChar() on the name, translateChar() on the input again, strncmp() and vmaxset(). x[[\"name\"]] translates and compares each name in get1index(), after do_subset2_dflt() has looked for 'drop' and 'exact' arguments and for dims.\n\nTimings: list-name-matching-bench.R, attached, prints ns per evaluation. On aarch64 macOS (Apple clang 21, r90888), l$j for the tenth of ten names goes from 78 to 21 ns and l[[\"j\"]] from 73 to 39; with non-ASCII names l[[\"name\"]] goes from 74 to 64.\n\nProposed patch: attached as list-name-matching.diff, against r90888. The input is translated once; a name that is the same CHARSXP as the input matches, and an ASCII name is compared without translateChar(), which returns such a string as it is. do_subset2_dflt() handles x[[\"name\"]] for a list without dims first. Results are unchanged, including partial matching and its warnings, names in other encodings and the error for a \"bytes\" name. eval.c is not touched.\n\nmake check-all passes in CI on Linux, macOS and Windows. The patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/494, with the full measurements.",
